Compute the global L2-sensitivity of f under the adjacency “differ in at most one entry.” Write f(X) = ∑i g(x_i) with g(x) = x^2(1−x)^2. On [0,1], g'(x) = 2x(1−x)(1−2x), so extrema at x ∈ {0, 1/2, 1}. We have g(0)=g(1)=0 and g(1/2)=1/16, hence range(g) = [0, 1/16]. For neighboring X, X' differing in one coordinate, |f(X)−f(X')| = |g(x)−g(y)| ≤ 1/16. Thus the (scalar) L2-sensitivity is Δ = 1/16.

The Gaussian mechanism adding N(0, σ^2) noise to a function with L2-sensitivity Δ is μ-GDP with μ = Δ/σ. Here σ^2 = (1/16)^2/β^2, so σ = Δ/β and consequently μ = Δ/σ = β. Therefore, the mechanism achieves β-Gaussian differential privacy.

